    <h1>The <span class="light">plagues that</span><br><span class="gold">broke the world</span></h1>
    <p class="hero-deck">Before antibiotics, before germ theory, before microscopes, infectious diseases regularly killed a third of a continent’s population in a single event. The Black Death alone killed more humans in four years than the First World War killed in four — and left the survivors living in a fundamentally different civilization. This is what we know, and what we finally understand, about the pandemics that shaped history.</p>
    <div class="hero-note"><b>A note on the pathogen:</b> The Black Plague was not caused by a virus. It was caused by the bacterium <em>Yersinia pestis</em>, transmitted from infected rodents to humans via flea bites — and, in its pneumonic form, directly between people. This distinction matters because bacteria respond to antibiotics: the same disease that killed 60% of medieval Europeans is now treatable with a course of doxycycline, if caught early.</div>
