<!-- ── Y. PESTIS — WHAT THE PATHOGEN IS ─────────────────────────────── -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-hd">
      <div class="t disp">The pathogen — <em>Yersinia pestis</em></div>
      <div class="sub">First identified 1894 · three lethal forms</div>
    </div>
    <div class="bio-grid">
      <div class="bio-block">
        <h4>What it is</h4>
        <p><em>Yersinia pestis</em> is a gram-negative bacterium in the family Yersiniaceae. It evolved from <em>Yersinia pseudotuberculosis</em>, a gut pathogen, through a small number of genetic mutations that gave it the ability to colonise fleas, spread to the lymphatic system, and overwhelm the immune response — changes that happened within the last 5,000 to 20,000 years on an evolutionary timescale.</p>
        <p>In nature the bacterium circulates silently within populations of wild rodents, causing them little harm. Transmission to humans happens through the bite of infected fleas, through contact with infected animal tissue, or — in the pneumonic form — directly between people through respiratory droplets. All three forms are the same bacterium, in different anatomical locations.</p>
        <div class="tags">
          <span class="tag">Gram-negative bacterium</span>
          <span class="tag">Family Yersiniaceae</span>
          <span class="tag">Zoonotic · rodent reservoir</span>
          <span class="tag">Flea-borne (primary)</span>
          <span class="tag">Airborne (pneumonic form)</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="bio-block">
        <h4>How it was confirmed — ancient DNA</h4>
        <p>The identification of <em>Y. pestis</em> as the cause of the Black Death was debated for over a century — some historians argued for anthrax, viral hemorrhagic fever, or even an unknown disease. The answer came from ancient DNA (aDNA) extracted from the teeth of burial victims.</p>
        <p>By the 1990s and 2000s, aDNA from plague pits in France, Germany, and England’s East Smithfield mass burial confirmed the bacterium. The 2022 Nature study went further — DNA from seven individuals at Kara-Djigach and Burana cemeteries in Kyrgyzstan, buried with tombstones dated 1338–1339 and inscribed “pestilence,” confirmed those graves hold the direct ancestral strain of the Black Death: the “Big Bang” of <em>Y. pestis</em> diversity that seeded all the major lineages that swept the medieval world.</p>
        <div class="tags">
          <span class="tag">aDNA from teeth · dental pulp</span>
          <span class="tag">Nature, June 2022</span>
          <span class="tag">Max Planck Institute · Univ. Tübingen</span>
          <span class="tag">Big Bang diversification event</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="form-row">
      <div class="form" style="border-top:2px solid var(--red)">
        <div class="fn">Bubonic plague</div>
        <div class="fc">Flea bite → bacteria enter lymphatic system → lymph nodes swell into painful swellings called “buboes” (Greek: groin). The most common form. Named the Black Death for the dark discolouration of necrotic skin.</div>
        <div class="cfr-bar"><div class="cfr-fill" style="width:55%;background:var(--red)"></div></div>
        <div class="cfr-val">CFR 30–60% · untreated · days to 2 weeks</div>
      </div>
      <div class="form" style="border-top:2px solid var(--red)">
        <div class="fn">Septicemic plague</div>
        <div class="fc">Bacteria enter the bloodstream directly, overwhelming it before any bubo can form. Rapid organ failure, internal bleeding, and black skin discolouration (acral gangrene) — the other explanation for the “Black” in Black Death. Can arise from flea bite or develop from bubonic.</div>
        <div class="cfr-bar"><div class="cfr-fill" style="width:95%;background:var(--red)"></div></div>
        <div class="cfr-val">CFR near 100% · untreated · hours to days</div>
      </div>
      <div class="form" style="border-top:2px solid var(--red)">
        <div class="fn">Pneumonic plague</div>
        <div class="fc">Bacteria infect the lungs — either from inhaling droplets from another person, or from bubonic/septicemic plague spreading to the lungs. The only form that spreads person to person, which is what makes it theoretically the most dangerous and what powered super-spreader events in medieval hospitals and cities.</div>
        <div class="cfr-bar"><div class="cfr-fill" style="width:98%;background:var(--red)"></div></div>
        <div class="cfr-val">CFR ~100% · untreated · within 36 hours</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ── CIVILIZATIONAL CONSEQUENCES ───────────────────────────────────── -->
<section class="section">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-hd">
      <div class="t disp">How the Black Death changed the world</div>
      <div class="sub">1350s onward</div>
    </div>
    <div class="cons-grid">
      <div class="con"><div class="icon">⚖️</div><div class="title">End of feudal labour</div><div class="body">A third to a half of the agricultural workforce was dead. Suddenly, surviving peasants could negotiate wages or simply leave — something inconceivable before. The feudal system that had organized European society for centuries never fully recovered.</div></div>
      <div class="con"><div class="icon">⛪</div><div class="title">The Church undermined</div><div class="body">The clergy died at the same rate as everyone else — despite their prayers. The inability of the Church to explain or prevent the catastrophe permanently fractured its authority over European intellectual life, accelerating the conditions for the Renaissance and later the Reformation.</div></div>
      <div class="con"><div class="icon">🔬</div><div class="title">First public health institutions</div><div class="body">Italian city-states invented the formal public health apparatus: Venice created the world’s first quarantine in 1377 (40-day isolation, “quarantina”). Milan built the first permanent board of health in 1450. These institutions are the direct ancestors of today’s CDC and WHO.</div></div>
      <div class="con"><div class="icon">💀</div><div class="title">Death in art and culture</div><div class="body">The danse macabre, memento mori, and a profound preoccupation with death entered European art and literature for the next two centuries. Boccaccio’s Decameron, Petrarch’s elegies, and later Chaucer’s Canterbury Tales were all direct responses to the plague experience.</div></div>
      <div class="con"><div class="icon">🌍</div><div class="title">Jewish communities massacred</div><div class="body">Across Germany, France, and the Holy Roman Empire, Jewish communities were blamed for poisoning wells and massacred in their hundreds of thousands — one of the worst pre-Holocaust persecutions in European history. Pope Clement VI issued two papal bulls stating Jews were dying of plague too and were not responsible, but it did little to stop the violence.</div></div>
      <div class="con"><div class="icon">🗣️</div><div class="title">English language emerged</div><div class="body">The Norman French-speaking ruling class died at similar rates to English-speaking commoners. Within a generation, the social pressure to speak French had collapsed, and English began its rise as the language of commerce, law, and eventually empire — a direct if unintended consequence of the plague’s social levelling.</div></div>
      <div class="con"><div class="icon">💰</div><div class="title">Wages and trade shifted</div><div class="body">A Europe with a third fewer people needed a third fewer things but still had the same capital — land, ships, tools, buildings. This compression of the labour-to-capital ratio, combined with the trade disruptions, is one of the structural reasons economic historians link the Black Death to the long-run rise of mercantile capitalism in the 15th and 16th centuries.</div></div>
      <div class="con"><div class="icon">🔭</div><div class="title">Accelerated the Renaissance</div><div class="body">Florence lost roughly 60% of its population. The survivors inherited concentrated wealth, disrupted hierarchies, and a visceral awareness of human fragility. The Renaissance’s individualism, its questioning of authority, its fascination with the body — each one traces at least partial roots to the civilizational rupture of 1348.</div></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

