      <div class="mod-stat"><div class="v">1894</div><div class="k">Year Y. pestis first identified · Yersin, Hong Kong</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="mod-body">
      <div class="mod-block">
        <h4>Why it still circulates</h4>
        <p><em>Yersinia pestis</em> is not a human disease that could be eradicated the way smallpox was. It lives in wild rodent populations — ground squirrels, prairie dogs, and chipmunks in the American Southwest; gerbils and rats across Central Asia and Africa — where it causes little harm and has persisted for millennia. As long as those reservoir populations exist, the bacterium does too. Modern rodent die-offs in plague-endemic zones are often the early warning that transmission to humans may follow.</p>
        <p>In the United States, plague cases cluster in New Mexico, Arizona, Colorado, and California — the descendants of a Third Pandemic introduction around 1900 that spread into the Western rodent population and has never been eradicated. Cases are typically traceable to contact with infected animals or their fleas during outdoor activities.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="mod-block">
        <h4>Treatment — what changed everything</h4>
        <p>Streptomycin, the first effective treatment, was identified by Selman Waksman in 1943 and is still used today. Doxycycline and ciprofloxacin are the current first-line choices. If treatment begins within 24 hours of symptom onset for pneumonic plague, or within a few days for bubonic, survival rates exceed 90%. The same disease that swept through medieval Europe killing 6 in 10 is now, in the context of access to antibiotics and rapid diagnosis, essentially curable.</p>
        <p>No vaccine is currently licensed for general use in most countries, though candidates exist. The US discontinued its commercial vaccine in 1999 due to limited demand. The plague’s continued presence in the world is a story about ecology, not medical inability — we simply cannot reach all the rodent reservoirs.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

